Deep Creative Reframings of LRC — opus-S540

======================================================================
REFRAME 1: BRAID — runner trajectories as a braid group
======================================================================

n=4, speeds=(1, 2, 3):
  observer-runner crossings per period: 12
  runner-runner crossings: 4
  total braid crossings: 16
  braid group: B_4 on 4 strands

  avg unlinked fraction: -0.5000 (NEGATIVE)
  (negative means observer is more often linked than not)

n=5, speeds=(1, 2, 3, 4):
  observer-runner crossings per period: 20
  runner-runner crossings: 10
  total braid crossings: 30
  braid group: B_5 on 5 strands

  avg unlinked fraction: -0.6000 (NEGATIVE)
  (negative means observer is more often linked than not)

n=6, speeds=(1, 2, 3, 4, 5):
  observer-runner crossings per period: 30
  runner-runner crossings: 20
  total braid crossings: 50
  braid group: B_6 on 6 strands

  avg unlinked fraction: -0.6667 (NEGATIVE)
  (negative means observer is more often linked than not)

n=7, speeds=(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6):
  observer-runner crossings per period: 42
  runner-runner crossings: 35
  total braid crossings: 77
  braid group: B_7 on 7 strands

  avg unlinked fraction: -0.7143 (NEGATIVE)
  (negative means observer is more often linked than not)

BRAID INSIGHT: the observer strand is 'entangled' with runner strands.
LRC says: the braid has a moment of 'disentanglement' where the
observer is free. The entanglement measure (linking number) is
always 0 algebraically, but the GEOMETRIC entanglement (crossing
pattern) determines whether disentanglement is possible.

The braid group B_n acts on the tournament: a crossing σ_i
corresponds to an arc flip in the tournament. The BRAID INVARIANTS
(Jones polynomial, etc.) might encode the LRC condition.

======================================================================
REFRAME 2: ECLIPSE — moving shadows covering the observer
======================================================================

n=4:
  shadow width: 2/4 = 0.5000
  total shadow measure: 3×2/4 = 1.5000
  eclipse excess: 0.5000
  avg pairwise overlap: 0.7500
  eclipse excess / overlap: 0.67

n=5:
  shadow width: 2/5 = 0.4000
  total shadow measure: 4×2/5 = 1.6000
  eclipse excess: 0.6000
  avg pairwise overlap: 0.9600
  eclipse excess / overlap: 0.63

n=6:
  shadow width: 2/6 = 0.3333
  total shadow measure: 5×2/6 = 1.6667
  eclipse excess: 0.6667
  avg pairwise overlap: 1.1111
  eclipse excess / overlap: 0.60

n=7:
  shadow width: 2/7 = 0.2857
  total shadow measure: 6×2/7 = 1.7143
  eclipse excess: 0.7143
  avg pairwise overlap: 1.2245
  eclipse excess / overlap: 0.58

n=14:
  shadow width: 2/14 = 0.1429
  total shadow measure: 13×2/14 = 1.8571
  eclipse excess: 0.8571
  avg pairwise overlap: 1.5918
  eclipse excess / overlap: 0.54

ECLIPSE INSIGHT: the total shadow measure is 2(n-1)/n < 2.
For permanent eclipse, shadows must cover [0,1) with this budget.
The 'eclipse excess' (total - 1) = (n-2)/n is the amount of shadow
that MUST overlap. If the overlap is FORCED to be more than the excess,
the shadows can't cover [0,1), proving LRC.

The CASCADE proof (S527) shows: the overlap IS forced to exceed the
excess for n≥7, because the image-wrapping condition creates mandatory
shadow overlaps. The 'wasted' shadow = the lonely time.

======================================================================
REFRAME 3: LATTICE — closest-vector to the lonely box
======================================================================

n=4:
  box half-width: (n-2)/(2n) = 0.2500
  box volume: ((n-2)/n)^3 = 0.125000
  lattice points: lcm = 6
  expected in box: 0.8
  ≈ 1 (tight)

n=5:
  box half-width: (n-2)/(2n) = 0.3000
  box volume: ((n-2)/n)^4 = 0.129600
  lattice points: lcm = 12
  expected in box: 1.6
  ≈ 1 (tight)

n=6:
  box half-width: (n-2)/(2n) = 0.3333
  box volume: ((n-2)/n)^5 = 0.131687
  lattice points: lcm = 60
  expected in box: 7.9
  ≈ 1 (tight)

n=7:
  box half-width: (n-2)/(2n) = 0.3571
  box volume: ((n-2)/n)^6 = 0.132810
  lattice points: lcm = 60
  expected in box: 8.0
  ≈ 1 (tight)

n=14:
  box half-width: (n-2)/(2n) = 0.4286
  box volume: ((n-2)/n)^13 = 0.134801
  lattice points: lcm = 360360
  expected in box: 48576.7
  >> 1 (easy)

LATTICE INSIGHT: LRC is a DIOPHANTINE APPROXIMATION problem.
The rational line must come within (n-2)/(2n) of the box center
in the L∞ norm. This is the VIEW OBSTRUCTION problem (Cusick 1973).

The COVERING RADIUS of the speed lattice determines LRC:
  μ ≤ (n-2)/(2n) iff LRC holds.

For the initial segment: the lattice is 'well-spread' (many points)
but the box hit count is LOW (6 for n=14) because the lattice points
cluster in specific regions of the torus, missing the box interior
but grazing its boundary.

======================================================================
REFRAME 4: CONFLICT HYPERGRAPH — overlapping close zones
======================================================================

n=4 (409 sets):
  avg coverage range: [1.4966, 1.5006]
  expected (uniform): 1.5000

n=5 (205 sets):
  avg coverage range: [1.5946, 1.5992]
  expected (uniform): 1.6000

n=6 (126 sets):
  avg coverage range: [1.6662, 1.6682]
  expected (uniform): 1.6667

HYPERGRAPH INSIGHT: the average coverage is always 2(n-1)/n.
But the DISTRIBUTION of coverage matters:
  - Uniform coverage: each time has ~2(n-1)/n runners close.
    Lonely probability ≈ Poisson with λ=2(n-1)/n.
  - Clustered coverage: some times have many close runners,
    others have few → more likely to have gaps (lonely times).

The initial segment has the MOST UNIFORM coverage (least clustered)
→ LEAST likely to have gaps → tightest case (lonely measure 0).
All other speed sets have MORE clustering → MORE gaps → easier LRC.

