REFRAME 5: MUSICAL CANON — when does the round resolve?
======================================================================

n=4, voices at tempos (1, 2, 3):
  beat frequencies: [1, 2]
  fundamental beat: 1
  resolution period (lcm of beats): 2
  resolution points per period: 2
  consonance threshold: 1/4 of the octave

n=5, voices at tempos (1, 2, 3, 4):
  beat frequencies: [1, 2, 3]
  fundamental beat: 1
  resolution period (lcm of beats): 6
  resolution points per period: 6
  consonance threshold: 1/5 of the octave

n=6, voices at tempos (1, 2, 3, 4, 5):
  beat frequencies: [1, 2, 3, 4]
  fundamental beat: 1
  resolution period (lcm of beats): 12
  resolution points per period: 12
  consonance threshold: 1/6 of the octave

n=7, voices at tempos (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6):
  beat frequencies: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
  fundamental beat: 1
  resolution period (lcm of beats): 60
  resolution points per period: 60
  consonance threshold: 1/7 of the octave

MUSICAL INSIGHT: LRC is about BEAT PATTERN ALIGNMENT.
Each pair of voices creates a beat. The beats interact to create
a complex rhythm. The 'resolution' is when ALL beats align at a
consonant phase — all voices are simultaneously far from unison.

The INITIAL SEGMENT has the richest beat structure (all integer
beat frequencies), which makes alignment hardest (tightest case).
Speed sets with coprime beat frequencies have SPARSER interactions,
making resolution easier.

The FORMAL GROUP F(x,y)=(x+y)/(1+xy) is the CONSONANCE LAW:
it adds dissonances hyperbolically. The rapidity = hyperbolic
distance = musical 'interval'. The lonely condition = all
intervals exceed the consonance threshold 0.5*ln(n-1) in rapidity.

======================================================================
SYNTHESIS: Five faces of LRC
======================================================================

LRC has (at least) five natural mathematical identities:

1. TOURNAMENT: observer source in a marked round tournament.
   → iso class reachability on the metagraph G_n
   → the 'inside/outside' polygon geometry

2. BRAID: disentanglement of the observer strand.
   → linking numbers and braid invariants
   → the crossing pattern determines lonely times

3. ECLIPSE: gaps in moving shadow coverage.
   → overlap budget: total shadow = 2(n-1)/n
   → mandatory overlap (cascade) forces gaps

4. LATTICE: closest vector to the lonely box.
   → covering radius of the speed sublattice
   → Diophantine approximation in the L∞ norm

5. CANON: resolution of a musical round.
   → beat frequency alignment
   → formal group as consonance law

Each reframing highlights a different OBSTRUCTION to permanent
non-loneliness:
  - Tournament: the metagraph is too connected
  - Braid: the linking is algebraically zero
  - Eclipse: the shadow budget is too small
  - Lattice: the box is too large
  - Canon: the beats must eventually align

THE UNIFYING PRINCIPLE: n-1 periodic constraints on a single
parameter (time t) cannot SIMULTANEOUSLY block a target of
measure ≈ e^{-2} ≈ 13.5% because the constraints INTERFERE
with each other — their overlaps waste budget, their phases
drift apart, their linking cancels, their resonances decohere.

The INITIAL SEGMENT is the unique CRITICAL POINT where the
interference is MAXIMALLY COHERENT — all phases align, all
overlaps minimize, all beat frequencies harmonize. Even at
this critical point, the target is barely reached (wall-only).
Any perturbation breaks the coherence and opens gaps.

